.TH CYAML 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
cyaml \- overview of the YAML 1.2 parser, serializer and DOM
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
cyaml parses YAML text into a tree of nodes that a program can read
and change, and writes the tree back out as YAML.  Each node is
reached through an opaque cyaml handle.  cyaml_parse(3) describes
which input the parser accepts and what limits it applies, and this
page gives the rules of the YAML language that don't belong to one
function.
.fi
.SH "NODE TYPES"
.nf
cyaml_type(3) gives the type of a node, as a cyaml_node_type_t:

  CYAML_NULL         null, ~, or an empty value
  CYAML_BOOL         true or false, read with cyaml_bool_val(3)
  CYAML_INTEGER      a long long, read with cyaml_int_val(3)
  CYAML_FLOAT        a double, read with cyaml_double_val(3)
  CYAML_STRING       a UTF-8 string, read with cyaml_str_val(3)
  CYAML_LIST         a sequence of child nodes
  CYAML_DICTIONARY   a mapping from a string key to a child node

Dictionary keys are plain strings and not nodes.  When the document
writes a key as a number, a boolean, null or a collection, the
dictionary keeps it as canonical text.  See cyaml_parse(3).
.fi
.SH "IMPLICIT TYPING"
.nf
A plain (unquoted, untagged) scalar gets its type from the YAML 1.2
core schema:

  ~, null, Null, NULL, or empty          CYAML_NULL
  true, True, TRUE                       CYAML_BOOL (true)
  false, False, FALSE                    CYAML_BOOL (false)
  decimal integer, 0x hex, 0o octal      CYAML_INTEGER
  decimal float, exponent notation       CYAML_FLOAT
  .inf, .Inf, .INF, with an optional
    + or - sign                          CYAML_FLOAT
  .nan, .NaN, .NAN                       CYAML_FLOAT
  anything else                          CYAML_STRING

Under YAML 1.2, yes, no, on and off are strings, and so are other
spellings of the special floats such as .INf, inf and nan.  A decimal
float is a float whatever its magnitude, so a value too large for a
double, such as 1e400 or -1.0e400, is an infinity, and a value too
small for one becomes the nearest subnormal double or zero.  The
explicit !!float tag reads its text the same way.  A decimal integer
literal outside the range of a long long becomes a CYAML_FLOAT (an
infinity when it is too large for a double as well), and it never
wraps.  The hex and octal forms follow the strict core schema: "0x"
followed by hex digits and "0o" followed by octal digits, with a
lowercase x or o and no sign.  So -0x1, +0x10 and -0o7 are strings,
and so is an unsigned hex or octal literal above LLONG_MAX such as
0x8000000000000000, because neither form has a float reading, and an
explicit !!int or !!float tag refuses such text.  A single-quoted or
double-quoted scalar is always a string, whatever its text.  A tag
can override all of these rules; see TAGS.
.fi
.SH "TAGS"
.nf
A tag belongs to its node.  It can be !!str, a custom !foo, a %TAG
shorthand, or a verbatim !<...>, and cyaml_node_tag(3) gives it back
in fully resolved form.  There are five scalar core-schema tags:
!!null, !!bool, !!int, !!float and !!str.  Each one forces its type on
a scalar of any style, in place of the type that the text alone
would give, so "!!str 42" is the string "42".  !!bool, !!int and
!!float check the text of their scalar and the parse fails when it
doesn't match, while !!null accepts any text.  A custom tag forces no
type, so the node keeps its implicit type.  A bare "!" is the
non-specific tag: a plain scalar under it is a string whatever its
text ("! 42" is the string "42"), a collection keeps its kind, and no
node carries the tag.  A written tag that resolves to "!" alone, such
as "!<!>", is a parse error.

The text of a written tag follows YAML 1.2 sections 5.6 and 6.9.1.
The content of a verbatim tag and the prefix of a %TAG directive may
hold only URI characters: ASCII letters and digits and the
punctuation -#;/?:@&=+$,_.!~*'()[].  The suffix of a shorthand tag
may hold the same set without '!', ',', '[' and ']'.  Any other byte,
such as a space, '{', '<', a quote or a byte of a character outside
ASCII, is a parse error that names it, so write it as a percent
escape ("!<a%20b>" is the tag "a b").  The percent escapes of a
verbatim tag, a shorthand suffix and a %TAG prefix are all decoded
the same way, so "!e!a" under the prefix "tag:x%21," and
"!<tag:x%21,a>" are both the tag "tag:x!,a".

A tag on a dictionary key types the key the same way, and the
dictionary keeps the canonical text of the typed key but not the
tag.  The serializers write a custom tag in its verbatim !<...> form
and never as a %TAG shorthand.  They don't write a core-schema tag,
because implicit typing gives the same type again; see
cyaml_serialize(3).  cyaml_node_set_tag(3) and cyaml_set(3) keep a
core-schema tag only as long as it names the type of its node.
.fi
.SH "SUPPORTED YAML"
.nf
Block mappings, with implicit keys ("key: value") and explicit keys
("? key" then ": value"), which one mapping can mix in any order.
The ':' of an explicit value counts as one only when a space, a tab,
a line end or the end of input follows it, so ":name: value" is an
ordinary entry whose key is ":name".  An implicit key sits on one
line, but an explicit key can continue over several lines.

Block sequences ("- item").  A sequence can be the value of a mapping
key, and it may then have the same indentation as that key.

Flow mappings ("{a: 1, b: 2}").  A bare key with no ':' gives a null
value, and so does a ':' with no value.  An explicit "? key: value"
entry is accepted, and its key can continue over several lines.

Flow sequences ("[a, b, c]").  A "key: value" pair in a flow sequence
is a mapping with one entry, so "[foo: bar]" equals "[{foo: bar}]".
An explicit "[? key: value]" entry is accepted.

Plain, single-quoted and double-quoted scalars, each of which can
continue over several lines: a line break becomes a space, and a
blank line becomes a newline.  A plain scalar never starts with an
indicator character, so "a: ]" and "- ,x" are parse errors outside a
flow collection.  Inside a flow collection a plain scalar ends at
every flow indicator, so "[a{b]" is a parse error, and such text has
to be quoted.  A single-quoted scalar has no escapes, and '' in it is
one quote.  A double-quoted scalar accepts all YAML escapes, \euXXXX
included.

Literal (|) and folded (>) block scalars, with the chomping indicators
|+ (keep), |- (strip) and | (clip, the default).

Anchors, aliases, tags and merge keys.  See below.

The %YAML directive, which may appear at most once in each document
and which is checked against the MAJOR.MINOR grammar.  A major
version other than 1 is a parse error, and any minor version is
accepted and read as YAML 1.2.  A '%' with no directive name after it
is a parse error, and a directive with an unknown name is ignored.

The %TAG directive, whose prefixes apply to the document that
declares them.

Documents with "---" and "..." markers, and streams of several
documents.  See cyaml_parse(3).

Comments, from '#' to the end of the line.

A cyaml string, anchor or tag cannot contain a null byte, because
each of them is a null-terminated C string, so encode such data
first, as base64 for example.  The parser doesn't decode a !!binary
tag.
.fi
.SH "ANCHORS AND ALIASES"
.nf
"&name" on a node, a mapping key included, makes that node an anchor,
and "*name" later in the same document refers to it.  The parser
replaces each alias with an independent deep copy of the anchored
node, so the tree has no shared nodes, and changing the copy doesn't
affect the original or the other way round.  Registering an anchor
copies nothing.  An anchored node that no alias names costs the same
as without the anchor, and each alias pays only for its own copy.
An anchor applies only within its own document of a stream.  A node
can have at most one anchor and one tag, in either order, and an
alias can have neither.

Every copy that an alias or a merge key makes counts against a fixed
expansion limit, so a small document cannot grow into a tree of
unlimited size.  cyaml_parse(3) gives the numbers.
.fi
.SH "MERGE KEYS"
.nf
A mapping entry with the key "<<" merges other mappings into the
mapping that holds it.  The key is a merge key in two cases:
  - It is a plain scalar with no tag.
  - It has the core merge tag, plain or quoted.  The core merge tag is
    !!merge, a %TAG shorthand of it, or the verbatim
    !<tag:yaml.org,2002:merge>.
A "<<" that is quoted and has no tag is an ordinary key, and so is a
"<<" with any other tag, such as !!str.  The rule is the same in every
notation:
  - block mappings and flow mappings
  - implicit keys and explicit keys
  - the one-entry mapping of a "- <<: value" or "[<<: value]" sequence
    entry.

The value of a merge key is one mapping or a sequence of mappings,
usually aliases; any other value is a parse error.

A mapping can hold several merge keys, and the parser expands all of
them.  A merge key never becomes a member of the mapping, so it never
replaces another merge key, and an ordinary "<<" member (a quoted or
tagged "<<") stays a member beside the merged ones, before or after
them.

The parser copies the members of each source into the mapping, with
the precedence rules of PyYAML:

  - A key that the mapping defines itself always keeps its own value,
    whether it comes before or after the merge keys.
  - Of two merge keys, the later one wins over the earlier one.
  - In a sequence of sources, an earlier source wins over a later
    source.

The merged members go in the place of the first merge key that brings
their key, after the explicit members in front of that merge key and
before the explicit members behind it.  The parser adds them source by
source, each source in its own member order.  For example:

    defaults: &d {timeout: 30, retries: 3}
    service:
      <<: *d
      timeout: 60

gives service the members retries: 3 and then timeout: 60, and

    m:
      p: 0
      <<: {x: 1, y: 2}
      q: 1
      <<: {y: 3, z: 4}

gives m the members p: 0, x: 1, y: 3, q: 1 and z: 4.
.fi
.SH "DUPLICATE KEYS"
.nf
A key that appears more than once in a mapping is accepted.  The last
value becomes the key's value and the key keeps the place of its
first occurrence, and the earlier value is freed without any report.
Common YAML parsers do the same, but YAML 1.2 doesn't require it, so
a program must not expect a YAML parser to find a duplicate key.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP"
.nf
cyaml_parse(3), cyaml_clone(3) and the cyaml_create_*(3) functions
give a tree that the caller owns and frees with cyaml_destroy(3).
cyaml_get(3), cyaml_list_get(3), cyaml_dictionary_get(3) and the
dictionary cursor give borrowed references, and the tree keeps
owning those nodes.  cyaml_list_push(3) and cyaml_dictionary_set(3)
take the child, and their pages name the one return code that leaves
it with the caller.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A tree is not thread safe, but several threads can read one tree at
the same time as long as no thread changes it.  Separate trees are
independent, so separate threads can parse and serialize at once.
.fi
